  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: Convection risks remain the primary focus in the \nupcoming 24 hour period, as there continues to be uncertainty on if \nwe can advect sufficient moisture northward, and provide enough\nfocus of lift. Visible satellite this afternoon shows two areas\nof interest, one area of ACCAS across northeastern South Dakota\nand west central Minnesota, driven by 700 mb winds over the \ncrest of the Ridge. A second area of ACCAS drifting eastward out\nof the Badlands area. At the surface, one concern has been a \nslower arrival of boundary layer moisture as surface dewpoints \ncontinue to linger in the upper 50s to lower 60s. Further south,\nmodels continue to overestimate dew points in NE/KS, providing \nsome doubt on if the lower 70 dewpoints advertised by \"high \ninstability\" models can be realized. \n\nThe projections into the evening remain slightly uncertain with \nseveral focusing mechanisms available.  A surface trough extending \nfrom NW Minnesota into NE South Dakota and central South Dakota will \nprovide some minor convergence (focused greater over NW Minnesota \nand eastern North Dakota).  A weaker pre-frontal trough in the NE \nPanhandle into the Sandhills could also serve as a focal point. \nFinally, a few storms may also develop over the Minnesota River \nvalley downstream of the Buffalo Ridge. Any storm that develops off \nof these features will likely produced an increased risk for 1-2\" \nhail, but also 60-70 mph winds as MLCAPE pushes 2000+ J/KG and \neffective shear climbs towards 40 knots.  Gradual east southeast \nstorm tracks within the mean flow are likely which could put \nportions of SW Minnesota and the MO River valley into a higher early \nevening risk. However the likely overestimation of surface dew \npoints, poor focus of convergence, and weak inhibition, may \nalso work to prohibit convection over a large part of the Tri-\nState area into the evening. \n\nFurther west, additional isolated to scattered convection may form \nover the western High Plains late this afternoon and evening \nslightly stronger synoptic lift enters the area.  One or more \nclusters of storms could reach the western CWA by mid-late evening, \nbringing risks lower risk for hail, but higher risks for a few \nstronger wind gusts. There continues to be some agreement in \nmesoscale and lower resolution guidance that a subtle wave crossing \nthe Dakotas could keep scattered convection risks moving through the \nCWA until at least 4am.  Weakening instability, and worsening lapse \nrates should result in fewer strong to severe storms by daybreak \nTuesday. All this to say, coverage of this activity remains highly \nin doubt, which will keep areal PoPs lower in general. \n\nTUESDAY: Latest guidance continues to trend the greatest risks for \nsevere convection southward on Tuesday.  Overnight convection, along \nwith the passing shortwave should stabilize the area\nconsiderably by daybreak, with a lingering marginal risks for \nsome crossover hail risks near 12Z. We may however see quite a \nbit of stratus behind this wave, suggesting cooler than normal \ntemperatures into the afternoon. \n\nWEDNESDAY-THURDAY: Upper troughing moving into Hudson Bay will \nsharpen the NW flow over the Plains slightly by mid-week. \nEC/GFS indicates vorticity crossing from the Pacific Northwest \nthrough the Northern Rockies early on Wednesday pushing mid-lvl \nlift towards the region early Wednesday. Differences on the \ntrack of this wave keep PoPs lower, with GEFS/GFS further \nnortheast, and the EC leading to more impact through central \nNebraska instead. Another wave, along with a weak frontal \nboundary approach Thursday afternoon. Depending on the amount of\ninhibition nearby, a few thunderstorms could form along this \nboundary late in the day. \n\nFRIDAY-SUNDAY: Mid-lvl heights rise and the ridge sharpens over the \nRockies as we move into next weekend.  This transition of mid-lvl \nflow could bring one final risk for elevated showers/TSRA early\nFriday, but generally quiet and dry conditions then develop \ninto the weekend. Temperatures however will fall more under the \ninfluence of Canadian high pressure in the Dakotas, suggesting \nhighs may stay in the upper 70s to lower 80s.",
